Statement I: True. When electric discharge is passed through gaseous hydrogen, $H_{2}$ molecules dissociate into hydrogen atoms. These atoms get excited and, on returning to lower energy levels, emit radiation of discrete frequencies , giving a line spectrum (as per NCERT description of atomic spectra). Statement II: True. Use the Rydberg formula (NCERT) for hydrogen-like species: $\hat{
u}=RZ^{2}( rac{1}{n_{1}^{2}}- rac{1}{n_{2}^{2}})$ (a) Second line of Balmer series of $He^{+}$ Balmer series means $n_{1}=2$ . Second line means transition $n_{2}=4 ightarrow n_{1}=2$ . For $He^{+}$ , $Z=2$ . $\hat{
u}_{He^{+}}=R(2)^{2}( rac{1}{2^{2}}- rac{1}{4^{2}})=4R( rac{1}{4}- rac{1}{16})=4R( rac{3}{16})= rac{3R}{4}$ (b) First line of Lyman series of hydrogen atom Lyman series means $n_{1}=1$ . First line means transition $n_{2}=2 ightarrow n_{1}=1$ . For hydrogen, $Z=1$ . $\hat{
u}_{H}=R(1)^{2}( rac{1}{1^{2}}- rac{1}{2^{2}})=R(1- rac{1}{4})= rac{3R}{4}$ So, $\hat{
u}_{He^{+}}=\hat{
u}_{H}$ , hence their frequencies are equal . Correct option: A Both Statement I and Statement II are true.

For hydrogen spectrum, the wave number is given by the Rydberg formula: $\hat{
u}=R( rac{1}{n_{1}^{2}}- rac{1}{n_{2}^{2}})$ where: $n_{1}$ = lower energy level $n_{2}$ = higher energy level $n_{2}>n_{1}$ Now let us find the first line of each series. 1. First line of Balmer series For Balmer series: $n_{1}=2$ The first line means transition from $n_{2}=3$ to $n_{1}=2$ . So, $\hat{
u}_{B}=R( rac{1}{2^{2}}- rac{1}{3^{2}})=R( rac{1}{4}- rac{1}{9})$ $\hat{
u}_{B}=R( rac{9-4}{36})= rac{5R}{36}$ 2. First line of Brackett series For Brackett series: $n_{1}=4$ The first line means transition from $n_{2}=5$ to $n_{1}=4$ . So, $\hat{
u}_{Br}=R( rac{1}{4^{2}}- rac{1}{5^{2}})=R( rac{1}{16}- rac{1}{25})$ $\hat{
u}_{Br}=R( rac{25-16}{400})= rac{9R}{400}$ 3. Required ratio $\hat{
u}_{B}:\hat{
u}_{Br}= rac{5R}{36}: rac{9R}{400}$ Cancel $R$ : $= rac{5}{36}: rac{9}{400}$ $= rac{5}{36} 	imes  rac{400}{9}$ So ratio: $\hat{
u}_{B}:\hat{
u}_{Br}= rac{2000}{324}= rac{500}{81}$ Now, $ rac{500}{81}= rac{5 	imes 100}{81}$ and $ rac{100}{81} \approx 1.23$ So, $\hat{
u}_{B}:\hat{
u}_{Br}=5:0.81$ Therefore, the correct answer is: $	ext{Option B}5:0.81$

Balmer series corresponds to transitions ending at $n_{1}=2$ . For hydrogen, the wave number is given by $\hat{
u}=R( rac{1}{n_{1}^{2}}- rac{1}{n_{2}^{2}}),n_{2}>n_{1}$ For Balmer series: $n_{1}=2$ , so $\hat{
u}=R( rac{1}{4}- rac{1}{n_{2}^{2}}),n_{2}=3,4,5,\dots$ Now compute the first few Balmer lines: For $n_{2}=3$ : $\hat{
u}=R( rac{1}{4}- rac{1}{9})=R( rac{9-4}{36})= rac{5R}{36}$ For $n_{2}=4$ : $\hat{
u}=R( rac{1}{4}- rac{1}{16})=R( rac{4-1}{16})= rac{3R}{16}$ For $n_{2}=5$ : $\hat{
u}=R( rac{1}{4}- rac{1}{25})=R( rac{25-4}{100})= rac{21R}{100}$ These three values $( rac{5R}{36}, rac{3R}{16}, rac{21R}{100})$ match Option A . Correct option: A

The wavenumber of a radiation is defined as the number of wavelengths per unit distance and is the reciprocal of the wavelength. Wavenumber is commonly represented in units of $cm^{-1}$ . First, convert the given wavelength from angstroms ( $\overset{o}{A}$ ) to centimeters (cm). We know that: $1\overset{o}{A}=10^{-8}	ext{cm}$ Given wavelength is 5800 $\overset{o}{A}$ : $5800\overset{o}{A}=5800 	imes 10^{-8}	ext{cm}$ Now calculate the wavenumber ( $\hat{
u}$ ) which is the reciprocal of the wavelength: $\hat{
u}= rac{1}{5800 	imes 10^{-8}	ext{cm}}$ Simplify the expression: $\hat{
u}= rac{1}{5800 	imes 10^{-8}	ext{cm}}= rac{10^{8}}{5800}cm^{-1}$ Now, divide the numerator by the denominator to calculate the precise value: $\hat{
u}= rac{10^{8}}{5800} \approx 1.724 	imes 10^{4}cm^{-1}$ Here, it is given that the wavenumber is in the form of $x 	imes 10cm^{-1}$ , so $x$ would be the value we calculated divided by 10: $x= rac{1.724 	imes 10^{4}}{10}=1724$ Thus, the integer answer for the value of $x$ is: 1724

